WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.709 --> 00:00:06.509
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:12.260
I apologize for my voice today, I am trapped
in this frail human body, and sometimes it

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.260
falters.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.220
But you remember from the previous episode,
the papers must go on.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.159
In the last few years, we have seen a bunch
of new AI-based techniques that were specialized

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:24.929
in generating new and novel images.

00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:29.779
This is mainly done through learning-based
techniques, typically a Generative Adversarial

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:35.530
Network, a GAN in short, which is an architecture
where a generator neural network creates new

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:41.550
images, and passes it to a discriminator network,
which learns to distinguish real photos from

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.530
these fake, generated images.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:49.269
These two networks learn and improve together,
so much so that many of these techniques have

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:55.440
become so realistic that we sometimes can’t
even tell they are synthetic images unless

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.250
we look really closely.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.269
You see some examples here from BigGAN, a
previous technique that is based on this architecture.

00:01:02.269 --> 00:01:07.340
Now, normally, if we are looking to generate
a specific human face, we have to generate

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:13.250
hundreds and hundreds of these images, and
our best bet is to hope that sooner or later,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.680
we’ll find something that we were looking
for.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.430
So, of course, scientists were interested
in trying to exert control over the outputs,

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:27.030
and with followup works, we can now kind of
control the appearance, but, in return, we

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.610
have to accept the pose in which they are
given.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:36.579
And, this new project is about teaching a
learning algorithm to separate pose from identity.

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:41.479
Now, that sounds kind of possible with proper
supervision.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:43.039
What does this mean exactly?

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:47.990
Well, we have to train these GANs on a large
number of images so they can learn what a

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.750
human face looks like, what landmarks to expect
and how to form them properly when generating

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.119
new images.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:02:00.549
However, when the input images are given with
different poses, we will normally need to

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:06.460
add additional information to the discriminator
that describes the rotations of these people

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.479
and objects.

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:12.760
Well, hold on to your papers, because that
is exactly what is not happening in this new

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.760
work.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.291
This paper proposes an architecture that contains
a 3D transform and a projection unit, you

00:02:19.291 --> 00:02:26.020
see them here with red and blue, and, these
help us in separating pose and identity.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.810
As a result, we have much finer artistic control
over these during image generation.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.610
That is amazing.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:39.450
So as you see here, it enables a really nice
workflow where we can also set up the poses.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.160
Don’t like the camera position for this
generated bedroom?

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.350
No problem.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.090
Need to rotate the chairs?

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.480
No problem.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.720
And we are not even finished yet, because
when we set up the pose correctly, we’re

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.170
not stuck with these images - we can also
choose from several different appearances.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:02.010
And all this comes from the fact that this
technique was able to learn the intricacies

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.010
of these objects.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.010
Love it.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:10.020
Now, it is abundantly clear that as we rotate
these cars, or change the camera viewpoint

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.150
for the bedroom, a flickering effect is still
present.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.950
And this, is how research works.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.300
We try to solve a new problem, one step at
a time.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.310
Then, we find flaws in the solution, and improve
upon that.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:28.270
As a result, we always say, two more papers
down the line, and we’ll likely have smooth

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.830
and creamy transitions between the images.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.600
The Lambda sponsorship spot is coming in a
moment, and I don’t know if you have noticed

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.790
at the start, but they were also part of this
research project as well.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:50.100
I think that is as relevant of a sponsor as
it gets.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:04:20.489
Thanks for watching and for 
your generous support, and I'll see you next

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:29.520
time!

